  "conclusion": "Ordinary 131-tick human replay materially improved sample efficiency under the support-aware local fitted-Q treatment, but did not satisfy P3. At the full matched budget, demonstration-assisted learning discovered authenticated terminals in 3/4 seeds while scratch discovered 0/4, increased useful training transitions per wall second by 21%, and increased useful decisions per wall second by 47%. Generated primitive actions improved the best replay-assisted candidate from tick 136 at the diagnostic budget to tick 135 at the full budget, so the learner was not merely replaying the recorded tape. It still failed to beat the tick-131 demonstration, and scratch still required demonstration support to succeed at this capacity. Generic executable-factor coverage did not produce a diagnostic scratch terminal. A subsequent terminal-support-aware cold-start correction increased useful decisions, useful training transitions, and visited states at the full scratch budget, but again found 0/4 terminals and reached the same roughly 1,496-distance plateau. Extending relative-heading primitives across 4/8/16/40 ticks further increased useful diagnostic evidence without producing a terminal or matching that depth. Cold-start acquisition and primitive duration coverage were defective but are not the terminal barrier.",
